# 14. SPECIAL SECURITY RULES

The design must clearly represent the following policies.

## Rule 1 — Least Privilege

Users receive only the permissions needed for their jobs.

Do not grant all permissions automatically to every manager.

## Rule 2 — Separation of Duties

High-risk business processes must be separable.

Examples:

- The user who creates a payment should not automatically approve it.
- The user who creates a stock adjustment should not automatically approve it.
- The user who prepares payroll should not automatically be authorized to approve and execute it.
- The user who enters a journal should not automatically approve restricted journal entries.

## Rule 3 — No Silent Deletion

Important posted transactions must not be physically deleted through standard screens.

Use:

- Cancellation.
- Reversal.
- Correction.
- Adjustment.

Require appropriate authorization and an audit trail.

## Rule 4 — Sensitive Financial Access

Separate:

- View financial summaries.
- View exact product costs.
- View profit margins.
- View account balances.
- Prepare payments.
- Approve payments.
- Execute payments.
- Post journal entries.
- Close accounting periods.

## Rule 5 — Sensitive HR Access

Separate:

- View employee profile.
- View personal documents.
- View salary.
- View bank details.
- Prepare payroll.
- Approve payroll.
- Execute payroll.

## Rule 6 — Data Isolation

An employee assigned to one warehouse must not access another warehouse's stock simply by changing a URL.

An employee assigned to one branch must not access another branch's records without authorization.

## Rule 7 — Permission Changes

Role changes and privilege escalation must:

- Require appropriate administrator authority.
- Be recorded in the audit log.
- Display a confirmation screen.
- Show a preview of affected users and permissions.

## Rule 8 — Temporary Permissions

Support temporary access with:

- Start date.
- Expiration date.
- Reason.
- Approver.
- Automatic expiry.
- Audit history.

## Rule 9 — Emergency Access

Support a restricted emergency access process.

It must require:

- Justification.
- Authorized approval.
- Limited duration.
- Limited permissions.
- Detailed audit logging.

